> What happens if you move the mouse once, wait a frame, move it again by 1 pixel towards any direction (without clicking), wait a frame again, then click on the frame after?

Also no luck, neither hover effect nor button press.

Just to be sure - what I added is this:

```gdscript
var motion_event = InputEventMouseMotion.new()
motion_event.window_id = get_viewport().get_window().get_window_id()
motion_event.position = Vector2(50, 10)
motion_event.global_position = Vector2(50, 10)
motion_event.relative = Vector2(50, 10)
motion_event.screen_relative = Vector2(50, 10)
motion_event.velocity = Vector2(50, 10)
motion_event.screen_velocity = Vector2(50, 10)

get_viewport().push_input(motion_event, false)

await get_tree().process_frame

var motion_event2 = InputEventMouseMotion.new()
motion_event2.window_id = get_viewport().get_window().get_window_id()
motion_event2.position = Vector2(51, 11)
motion_event2.global_position = Vector2(51, 11)
motion_event2.relative = Vector2(1, 1)
motion_event2.screen_relative = Vector2(1, 1)
motion_event2.velocity = Vector2(1, 1)
motion_event2.screen_velocity = Vector2(1, 1)

get_viewport().push_input(motion_event2, false)

await get_tree().process_frame
```

I also defined a custom `func _notification(what: int) -> void:` where I printed the notification to make sure that no mouse enter notification is produced.

Is there anything we're missing which is required for a `NOTIFICATION_MOUSE_ENTER` to be produced?

After some more digging, I did find this in `viewport.cpp`:

```cpp
void Viewport::_update_mouse_over() {
	[...]

	if (get_tree()->get_root()->is_embedding_subwindows() || is_sub_viewport()) {
		// Use embedder logic for calculating mouse position.
		_update_mouse_over(gui.last_mouse_pos);
	} else {
		// Native Window: Use DisplayServer logic for calculating mouse position.
		Window *receiving_window = get_tree()->get_root()->gui.windowmanager_window_over;
		if (!receiving_window) {
			return;
		}

		Vector2 pos = DisplayServer::get_singleton()->mouse_get_position() - receiving_window->get_position();
		pos = receiving_window->get_final_transform().affine_inverse().xform(pos);

		receiving_window->_update_mouse_over(pos);
	}
}
```

Note the line in the block where subwindows are not embedded (as is required for this bug):

```cpp
Vector2 pos = DisplayServer::get_singleton()->mouse_get_position() - receiving_window->get_position();
```

Am I understanding correctly that this function is querying the mouse position from the DisplayServer, i.e. the operating system, rather than the internal GUI - meaning that any custom mouse events would be ignored here? That would explain the behavior - and it'd mean that this issue is related to https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/89109 (and maybe others).

It also makes sense, then, that @Sauermann's trick worked: 

> (Try selecting the second button, moving the mouse over the first button and activating the second button with the "space" key on the keyboard in your MRP)

I could be entirely off though, this is the first time I'm digging through this code 😄
